1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,600
<font color="#000000">We present DS-1000,</font>

2
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:08,780
<font color="#000000">a natural and reliable benchmark for data science
code generation.</font>

3
00:00:08,860 --> 00:00:12,960
<font color="#000000">DS-1000 gathers a thousand real problems across
seven widely used Python libraries such as NumPy and Pandas,</font>

4
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:17,140
<font color="#000000">all drawn from StackOverflow.</font>

5
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:21,320
<font color="#000000">It pairs these realistic problems with reliable
execution-based evaluation and a built-in</font>

6
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:25,500
<font color="#000000">defense against memorization,</font>

7
00:00:25,580 --> 00:00:29,680
<font color="#000000">giving the community a trustworthy yardstick for
data science coding models.</font>

8
00:00:30,060 --> 00:00:32,960
<font color="#000000">Data science coding is central to many fields,</font>

9
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:38,984
<font color="#000000">yet it demands fluency in specialized libraries
that create real barriers for everyday users.</font>

10
00:00:39,064 --> 00:00:42,223
<font color="#000000">Code generation models could lower those barriers,</font>

11
00:00:42,303 --> 00:00:46,240
<font color="#000000">but the community lacked a benchmark to measure
real progress.</font>

12
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:52,199
<font color="#000000">Most existing datasets focus on competitive or
interview-style problems that test algorithms</font>

13
00:00:52,279 --> 00:00:54,078
<font color="#000000">rather than real-world usage,</font>

14
00:00:54,158 --> 00:01:01,398
<font color="#000000">and many score answers with surface-form metrics
like BLEU that drift away from what programmers actually intend.</font>

15
00:01:01,478 --> 00:01:06,904
<font color="#000000">There was no benchmark combining natural, richly
contextual problems with a reliable,</font>

16
00:01:06,984 --> 00:01:09,560
<font color="#000000">execution-based way to judge correctness.</font>

17
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:14,526
<font color="#000000">Real data science questions rarely look like clean
textbook prompts.</font>

18
00:01:14,606 --> 00:01:19,123
<font color="#000000">On StackOverflow, users describe messy contexts:
their broken code,</font>

19
00:01:19,203 --> 00:01:24,131
<font color="#000000">the error they hit, and concrete input-output
examples of what they want.</font>

20
00:01:24,211 --> 00:01:27,013
<font color="#000000">Prior benchmarks strip that richness away.</font>

21
00:01:27,093 --> 00:01:29,689
<font color="#000000">At the same time, as models get better,</font>

22
00:01:29,769 --> 00:01:32,983
<font color="#000000">surface-form scores like BLEU become misleading,</font>

23
00:01:33,063 --> 00:01:36,345
<font color="#000000">rewarding text that looks right but does not run.</font>

24
00:01:36,425 --> 00:01:43,000
<font color="#000000">This motivates a benchmark built from natural
problems and judged by actually executing the code.</font>

25
00:01:43,380 --> 00:01:47,342
<font color="#FFFFFF">DS-1000 makes three contributions.</font>

26
00:01:47,422 --> 00:01:51,384
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, it provides a thousand realistic problems
with diverse contexts,</font>

27
00:01:51,464 --> 00:01:55,426
<font color="#FFFFFF">adapted from naturally occurring StackOverflow
questions across seven popular Python libraries.</font>

28
00:01:55,506 --> 00:01:59,468
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

29
00:01:59,548 --> 00:02:03,510
<font color="#FFFFFF">it implements reliable multi-criteria evaluation
that runs test cases and checks surface-form constraints,</font>

30
00:02:03,590 --> 00:02:07,552
<font color="#FFFFFF">so accepted solutions are almost always genuinely correct.</font>

31
00:02:07,632 --> 00:02:11,594
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

32
00:02:11,674 --> 00:02:15,636
<font color="#FFFFFF">it proactively defends against memorization by
perturbing problems so models cannot simply</font>

33
00:02:15,716 --> 00:02:19,678
<font color="#FFFFFF">recall pre-training answers.</font>

34
00:02:19,758 --> 00:02:23,720
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors release the benchmark and use it to
evaluate five state-of-the-art code models.</font>

35
00:02:24,100 --> 00:02:27,131
<font color="#000000">DS-1000 is built through a five-stage pipeline.</font>

36
00:02:27,211 --> 00:02:30,573
<font color="#000000">Annotators first select high-vote, testable, useful,</font>

37
00:02:30,653 --> 00:02:35,272
<font color="#000000">and representative StackOverflow problems and
rewrite them for clarity.</font>

38
00:02:35,352 --> 00:02:41,692
<font color="#000000">They add a code context with insertion markers
showing exactly where the model must fill in code.</font>

39
00:02:41,772 --> 00:02:47,451
<font color="#000000">They then implement automatic tests that check
functional correctness by executing test</font>

40
00:02:47,531 --> 00:02:50,628
<font color="#000000">cases and also enforce surface-form constraints,</font>

41
00:02:50,708 --> 00:02:54,798
<font color="#000000">such as forbidding certain APIs or keywords in the
syntax tree.</font>

42
00:02:54,878 --> 00:02:59,232
<font color="#000000">To defend against memorization, they perturb each
original problem,</font>

43
00:02:59,312 --> 00:03:06,778
<font color="#000000">using surface perturbations that leave the
reference solution unchanged and semantic perturbations that change it,</font>

44
00:03:06,858 --> 00:03:09,227
<font color="#000000">plus deliberately difficult rewrites.</font>

45
00:03:09,307 --> 00:03:14,720
<font color="#000000">Finally they red-team the evaluation, requiring it
to reject known-wrong solutions.</font>

46
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:20,280
<font color="#000000">Every problem, solution, and metric is reviewed by
at least three expert annotators.</font>

47
00:03:20,660 --> 00:03:27,690
<font color="#000000">DS-1000 contains a thousand problems originating
from four hundred fifty-one unique StackOverflow posts,</font>

48
00:03:27,770 --> 00:03:33,159
<font color="#000000">spanning seven widely used Python libraries: NumPy,
Pandas, TensorFlow, PyTorch,</font>

49
00:03:33,239 --> 00:03:35,620
<font color="#000000">SciPy, Scikit-learn, and Matplotlib.</font>

50
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:41,500
<font color="#000000">More than half of the problems are modified from
their sources to resist memorization,</font>

51
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:45,191
<font color="#000000">including one hundred fifty-two surface perturbations,</font>

52
00:03:45,271 --> 00:03:48,404
<font color="#000000">two hundred thirty-five semantic perturbations,</font>

53
00:03:48,484 --> 00:03:51,481
<font color="#000000">and one hundred sixty-two difficult rewrites.</font>

54
00:03:51,561 --> 00:03:56,813
<font color="#000000">On average each problem has one point six test
cases, one hundred forty words,</font>

55
00:03:56,893 --> 00:04:00,231
<font color="#000000">and a reference solution of three point six lines,</font>

56
00:04:00,311 --> 00:04:03,855
<font color="#000000">and about one in five carry surface-form constraints.</font>

57
00:04:03,935 --> 00:04:07,068
<font color="#000000">With a hundred forty average words per problem,</font>

58
00:04:07,148 --> 00:04:11,580
<font color="#000000">DS-1000 is far richer in context than other data
science datasets.</font>

59
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:15,779
<font color="#000000">DS-1000 clearly separates models of different strength.</font>

60
00:04:15,859 --> 00:04:19,890
<font color="#000000">The best public system, Codex-002 in the insertion format,</font>

61
00:04:19,970 --> 00:04:24,285
<font color="#000000">reaches only forty-three point three percent
average accuracy,</font>

62
00:04:24,365 --> 00:04:29,460
<font color="#000000">nontrivial but far from solved, leaving substantial
room for improvement.</font>

63
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:32,224
<font color="#000000">Across five models from three families,</font>

64
00:04:32,304 --> 00:04:38,179
<font color="#000000">accuracy ranges from seven point four percent up to
forty-three point three percent.</font>

65
00:04:38,259 --> 00:04:41,865
<font color="#000000">Insertion format, which supplies right-hand context,</font>

66
00:04:41,945 --> 00:04:46,827
<font color="#000000">gives Codex-002 a four point one percent boost over
completion format,</font>

67
00:04:46,907 --> 00:04:51,080
<font color="#000000">underscoring the value of infilling for data
science coding.</font>

68
00:04:51,160 --> 00:04:57,460
<font color="#000000">Weaker models such as CodeGen-6B and InCoder-6B
fall below five percent on some libraries.</font>

69
00:04:57,840 --> 00:04:59,975
<font color="#000000">To show why memorization matters,</font>

70
00:05:00,055 --> 00:05:03,466
<font color="#000000">the authors probe the popular numpy-100 problem set.</font>

71
00:05:03,546 --> 00:05:07,091
<font color="#000000">Codex-002 scores seventy-two point five percent there,</font>

72
00:05:07,171 --> 00:05:11,723
<font color="#000000">but accuracy collapses to forty point six percent
after perturbation,</font>

73
00:05:11,803 --> 00:05:17,161
<font color="#000000">and in thirty-six percent of semantic cases the
model still returns the original,</font>

74
00:05:17,241 --> 00:05:18,571
<font color="#000000">now-incorrect answer,</font>

75
00:05:18,651 --> 00:05:23,404
<font color="#000000">evidence that it is recalling memorized solutions
rather than reasoning.</font>

76
00:05:23,484 --> 00:05:28,640
<font color="#000000">On DS-1000 the perturbation drop is much gentler,
about three to nine percent,</font>

77
00:05:28,720 --> 00:05:31,862
<font color="#000000">because these problems appear less often online.</font>

78
00:05:31,942 --> 00:05:37,770
<font color="#000000">This confirms perturbation as a practical defense
against memorization by future models.</font>

79
00:05:38,150 --> 00:05:40,151
<font color="#000000">A few numbers capture DS-1000.</font>

80
00:05:40,231 --> 00:05:43,688
<font color="#000000">It offers a thousand problems over seven libraries,</font>

81
00:05:43,768 --> 00:05:47,919
<font color="#000000">drawn from four hundred fifty-one unique
StackOverflow posts.</font>

82
00:05:47,999 --> 00:05:52,705
<font color="#000000">The best model reaches only forty-three point three
percent accuracy.</font>

83
00:05:52,785 --> 00:05:57,283
<font color="#000000">The evaluation is highly reliable: among all
solutions it accepts,</font>

84
00:05:57,363 --> 00:06:00,890
<font color="#000000">just one point eight percent are actually incorrect,</font>

85
00:06:00,970 --> 00:06:05,398
<font color="#000000">and only about half a percent of rejected ones are
truly correct.</font>

86
00:06:05,478 --> 00:06:09,144
<font color="#000000">And with a hundred forty words per problem on average,</font>

87
00:06:09,224 --> 00:06:12,820
<font color="#000000">its contexts are far richer than comparable datasets.</font>

88
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:17,998
<font color="#FFFFFF">DS-1000 gives the community a data science code
benchmark that is realistic,</font>

89
00:06:18,078 --> 00:06:21,207
<font color="#FFFFFF">reliably evaluated, and resistant to memorization.</font>

90
00:06:21,288 --> 00:06:24,802
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because it is built from natural StackOverflow problems,</font>

91
00:06:24,882 --> 00:06:29,488
<font color="#FFFFFF">judged by executing code against test cases and
surface-form constraints,</font>

92
00:06:29,568 --> 00:06:34,943
<font color="#FFFFFF">and defended by deliberate perturbation, its scores
are trustworthy and hard to game.</font>

93
00:06:35,023 --> 00:06:40,207
<font color="#FFFFFF">And because even the strongest model reaches only
forty-three point three percent,</font>

94
00:06:40,287 --> 00:06:45,470
<font color="#FFFFFF">it makes clear how much room remains for progress
in data science code generation.</font>
